The fuzzer didn't crash. Let me trace through the code more carefully. The issue might be that the fuzzer's HTTP serving path isn't being triggered, or the chunk data isn't reaching `deliver_chunked_chunks` correctly.

Let me look at the fuzzer code again:

```c
if (size > 0) {
    struct mg_tcpip_if mif = {...};
    struct mg_mgr mgr;
    mg_mgr_init(&mgr);
    mg_tcpip_init(&mgr, &mif);

    void *pkt = malloc(size);
    struct eth *eth = (struct eth *) pkt;
    memcpy(pkt, data, size);
    if (size > sizeof(*eth)) {
      static size_t i;
      uint16_t eth_types[] = {0x800, 0x800, 0x806, 0x86dd};
      memcpy(eth->dst, mif.mac, 6);
      eth->type = mg_htons(eth_types[i++]);
      if (i >= sizeof(eth_types) / sizeof(eth_types[0])) i = 0;
    }

    mg_tcpip_rx(&mif, pkt, size);

    // Test HTTP serving
    const char *url = "http://localhost:12345";
    struct mg_connection *c = mg_http_connect(&mgr, url, fn, NULL);
    mg_iobuf_add(&c->recv, 0, data, size);
    c->pfn(c, MG_EV_READ, NULL, NULL);

    mg_mgr_free(&mgr);
    free(pkt);
    mg_tcpip_free(&mif);
}
```

So the fuzzer:
1. First calls `mg_tcpip_rx(&mif, pkt, size)` with the data as a network packet.
2. Then creates an HTTP connection and adds the data to `c->recv`, then calls `c->pfn(c, MG_EV_READ, NULL, NULL)`.

The `c->pfn` for an HTTP connection is `http_cb`. So `http_cb(c, MG_EV_READ, NULL, NULL)` is called.

In `http_cb`:
```c
if (ev == MG_EV_READ || ev == MG_EV_CLOSE) {
    struct mg_http_message hm;
    while (c->recv.buf != NULL && c->recv.len > 0) {
      bool next = false;
      int hlen = mg_http_parse((char *) c->recv.buf, c->recv.len, &hm);
      if (hlen < 0) {
        mg_error(c, "HTTP parse:\n%.*s", (int) c->recv.len, c->recv.buf);
        break;
      }
      if (c->is_resp) break;
      if (hlen == 0) break;
      if (ev == MG_EV_CLOSE) {
        hm.message.len = c->recv.len;
        hm.body.len = hm.message.len - (size_t) (hm.body.ptr - hm.message.ptr);
      }
      if (mg_is_chunked(&hm)) {
        deliver_chunked_chunks(c, (size_t) hlen, &hm, &next);
      } else {
        deliver_normal_chunks(c, (size_t) hlen, &hm, &next);
      }
      ...
```

So `mg_http_parse` is called on the data. Let me check if `mg_http_parse` succeeds with my PoC. The issue might be that `mg_http_parse` fails because of the chunk data.

Let me check `mg_http_get_request_len` with my data. The data is:
```
GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\r\n\r\nFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\r\n\n
```

`mg_http_get_request_len` looks for `\n\n` or `\n\r\n`. The headers end with `\r\n\r\n`. So `req_len` would be the position after `\r\n\r\n`.

Let me count: `GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n` = 17 bytes, `Transfer-Encoding: chunked\r\n` = 29 bytes, `\r\n` = 2 bytes. Total = 48 bytes. So `req_len = 48`.

Then `end = s + 48`. The chunk data starts at position 48: `FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\r\n\n`.

In `mg_http_parse`:
- Parse method: `GET`.
- Parse URI: `/`.
- Parse protocol: `HTTP/1.1`.
- Parse headers: `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`.
- `mg_http_get_header(hm, "Content-Length")` - not present.
- `is_response = false` (method is GET).
- `body.len == ~0 && !is_response && method != PUT && method != POST` - true, so `body.len = 0`, `message.len = req_len = 48`.

Returns `req_len = 48`.

Then in `http_cb`:
- `mg_is_chunked(&hm)` - checks `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`. Returns true.
- `deliver_chunked_chunks(c, 48, &hm, &next)` is called.

In `deliver_chunked_chunks`:
- `buf = &c->recv.buf[48]` - points to `FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\r\n\n`.
- `len = c->recv.len - 48` = `65 - 48` = `17`.
- `processed = 0`.
- `getchunk(mg_str_n(buf + 0, 17), &pl, &dl)` is called.

In `getchunk`:
- `s` = `FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\r\n\n` (17 bytes).
- `i` counts until `\r` or `\n`: `i = 16`.
- `n = mg_unhexn(s.ptr, 16)` = `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`.
- `i + n + 4` = `16 + 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF + 4` = `19` (wraps).
- `s.len < 19` - `17 < 19` is TRUE! So it returns false.

Ah, that's the problem. `s.len` is 17, which is less than 19. So `getchunk` returns false. I need `s.len >= 19`.

The chunk data needs to be at least 19 bytes. Let me add more data after the chunk. The chunk data is `FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\r\n\n` (19 bytes). But `s.len` is `c->recv.len - hlen`. So I need `c->recv.len >= 48 + 19 = 67`.

Let me add padding after the chunk data. Let me update the PoC.

